  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Daily chances for strong storms with possible flash flooding \ntoday through Monday. Hot temperatures are expected on Monday\nacross the VA/NC Piedmonts. \n\n\nThe large scale pattern continues to maintain an upper level\nridge across the northern Gulf states, and an upper level \ntrough over the northeast with convective perturbations moving \nfrom the Midwest east into the Ohio Valley, and into West\nVirginia/Virginia. At the surface, a stationary front continues\nto remain across portions of West Virginia and Virginia, and\nremains a focal point for afternoon and evening storm\ndevelopment. Current CAM guidance has scattered convection\ndeveloping during the afternoon hours in an environment with\nroughly 20 knots of 0-6km deep layer shear, 2000 J/Kg of \nSBCAPE, and 500-700 J/Kg of DCAPE. This type of environment will\nbe capable of producing strong damaging wind gusts; however,\nstorm nature will predominantly remain multicellular clusters \nas they track through the area. along with the severe threat, \nPWATs continue to remain extremely high, with 1.5-2.0 inch PWATs\nforecast for this afternoon. Values this high will be conducive\nfor flash flooding potential across the region with any strong \nstorms that may remain over the same location or continuously \ntrack over the same locations. \n\nSome CAM guidance has a late evening MCS moving across central\nWest Virginia and into central Virginia along the I-64 corridor\nlate tonight into the overnight hours. While there is still a\nlot of uncertainty in this occurring, there have been a couple\nCAMs that have shown this solution. This would increase any\nflash flood and severe weather potential across this area if\nthis does occur. No flood watches will be issued at this time\ngiven the uncertainty, but could very well be issued in\nsubsequent updates if this CAM guidance continues this trend\ngiven the potential for training storms.\n\n\nNot much change in the forecast as scattered showers and\nthunderstorms look possible again on Sunday with ridge building\na little more. The stationary front finally lifts north as a\nwarm front on Sunday before a cold front pushes through the area\nMonday afternoon and evening. As the upper level ridge nudges\neast on Sunday and Monday, temperatures will continue to rise, \nwith near Heat Advisory criteria levels possible across the \nPiedmont of Virginia and North Carolina on Sunday and Monday. By\nMonday evening the ridge gets flattened with more shortwaves \nmoving across the area once again. The flow being more westerly \nmay keep storm coverage for east of the mountains somewhat \nlimited.",
